1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,729
<font color="#FFFFFF">Mixture models usually blend simple distributions
by adding them.</font>

2
00:00:04,809 --> 00:00:08,640
<font color="#FFFFFF">But what if a mixture could also subtract
probability mass?</font>

3
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:10,164
<font color="#FFFFFF">Published at ICLR 2024,</font>

4
00:00:10,244 --> 00:00:15,004
<font color="#FFFFFF">this paper shows how to learn deep subtractive
mixtures by squaring them.</font>

5
00:00:15,084 --> 00:00:20,373
<font color="#FFFFFF">Squaring keeps the model a valid distribution while
allowing negative parameters,</font>

6
00:00:20,453 --> 00:00:26,472
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the authors prove these squared non-monotonic
circuits can be exponentially more compact</font>

7
00:00:26,552 --> 00:00:28,593
<font color="#FFFFFF">than ordinary additive mixtures,</font>

8
00:00:28,673 --> 00:00:34,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">then confirm the gain on real-world density
estimation and language-model distillation.</font>

9
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:40,662
<font color="#FFFFFF">Traditional mixture models build complex
distributions by blending simple ones additively.</font>

10
00:00:40,742 --> 00:00:43,663
<font color="#FFFFFF">That works, but it can be wildly inefficient.</font>

11
00:00:43,743 --> 00:00:47,597
<font color="#FFFFFF">If the target distribution has gaps or holes in its
domain,</font>

12
00:00:47,677 --> 00:00:52,932
<font color="#FFFFFF">an additive mixture must stack up many components
just to carve those holes out.</font>

13
00:00:53,012 --> 00:00:59,201
<font color="#FFFFFF">The natural fix is to let some components subtract
probability mass instead of only adding it.</font>

14
00:00:59,281 --> 00:01:01,735
<font color="#FFFFFF">The catch: once you allow subtraction,</font>

15
00:01:01,815 --> 00:01:06,069
<font color="#FFFFFF">the model can dip below zero and stop being a valid
distribution,</font>

16
00:01:06,149 --> 00:01:08,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">and learning it becomes genuinely hard.</font>

17
00:01:09,050 --> 00:01:10,578
<font color="#FFFFFF">So why revisit this now?</font>

18
00:01:10,658 --> 00:01:18,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because subtraction pays off: a mixture that can
cancel mass captures complex shapes with far fewer components.</font>

19
00:01:18,097 --> 00:01:21,904
<font color="#FFFFFF">The problem is guaranteeing the result stays non-negative.</font>

20
00:01:21,984 --> 00:01:25,992
<font color="#FFFFFF">Energy-based models enforce non-negativity by
exponentiation,</font>

21
00:01:26,072 --> 00:01:28,539
<font color="#FFFFFF">but then lose tractable normalization.</font>

22
00:01:28,619 --> 00:01:31,152
<font color="#FFFFFF">Meanwhile several separate communities,</font>

23
00:01:31,232 --> 00:01:35,307
<font color="#FFFFFF">from signal processing to kernel methods to quantum
mechanics,</font>

24
00:01:35,387 --> 00:01:40,869
<font color="#FFFFFF">each rediscovered a squaring trick to enforce
non-negativity, but without a common,</font>

25
00:01:40,949 --> 00:01:43,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">tractable framework tying them together.</font>

26
00:01:43,930 --> 00:01:47,642
<font color="#FFFFFF">This work makes four contributions.</font>

27
00:01:47,722 --> 00:01:51,434
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, a general framework for representing
subtractive mixtures by squaring,</font>

28
00:01:51,514 --> 00:01:55,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">expressed in the language of tensorized
probabilistic circuits.</font>

29
00:01:55,305 --> 00:01:59,017
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

30
00:01:59,097 --> 00:02:02,809
<font color="#FFFFFF">it shows these squared non-monotonic circuits
generalize several apparently different models with negative parameters,</font>

31
00:02:02,889 --> 00:02:06,601
<font color="#FFFFFF">including square-root-of-density models, positive
semi-definite kernel models,</font>

32
00:02:06,681 --> 00:02:10,393
<font color="#FFFFFF">and Born machines from quantum physics.</font>

33
00:02:10,473 --> 00:02:14,185
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

34
00:02:14,265 --> 00:02:17,976
<font color="#FFFFFF">it proves an exponential lower bound: functions a
single squared circuit encodes compactly</font>

35
00:02:18,056 --> 00:02:21,768
<font color="#FFFFFF">need exponentially more units in any monotonic circuit.</font>

36
00:02:21,848 --> 00:02:25,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">And fourth, it backs this with experiments on
real-world data.</font>

37
00:02:25,940 --> 00:02:29,895
<font color="#FFFFFF">Here is the core idea.</font>

38
00:02:29,975 --> 00:02:33,929
<font color="#FFFFFF">Take a mixture with unconstrained real weights,</font>

39
00:02:34,009 --> 00:02:37,964
<font color="#FFFFFF">so components can have negative coefficients, and
simply square the whole thing.</font>

40
00:02:38,044 --> 00:02:41,998
<font color="#FFFFFF">Squaring forces the output to be non-negative no
matter what the weights are.</font>

41
00:02:42,078 --> 00:02:46,033
<font color="#FFFFFF">Expanding the square turns a K-component mixture
into a sum over all pairs of components,</font>

42
00:02:46,113 --> 00:02:50,067
<font color="#FFFFFF">a product-of-experts form whose partition function
can still be computed in closed form for many families.</font>

43
00:02:50,147 --> 00:02:54,102
<font color="#FFFFFF">To go deep,</font>

44
00:02:54,182 --> 00:02:58,136
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors square a tensorized
structured-decomposable circuit layer by layer.</font>

45
00:02:58,216 --> 00:03:02,171
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each squared layer holds a quadratic number of
units but still outputs a vector,</font>

46
00:03:02,251 --> 00:03:06,205
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the whole model trains efficiently with gradient
descent,</font>

47
00:03:06,285 --> 00:03:10,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">computing the normalizer just once per batch.</font>

48
00:03:10,620 --> 00:03:13,365
<font color="#FFFFFF">The experiments cover three regimes.</font>

49
00:03:13,445 --> 00:03:19,092
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, two-dimensional synthetic densities, both
continuous and discrete,</font>

50
00:03:19,172 --> 00:03:25,839
<font color="#FFFFFF">where you can literally see how well each model
captures rings and other holey shapes.</font>

51
00:03:25,919 --> 00:03:32,116
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, five standard UCI datasets: Power, Gas,
Hepmass, MiniBooNE, and BSDS300.</font>

52
00:03:32,196 --> 00:03:37,530
<font color="#FFFFFF">And third, distilling sentences sampled from the
GPT2 language model.</font>

53
00:03:37,610 --> 00:03:43,728
<font color="#FFFFFF">Baselines span full-covariance Gaussians,
normalizing flows like RealNVP, MADE,</font>

54
00:03:43,808 --> 00:03:50,240
<font color="#FFFFFF">MAF and NSF, monotonic probabilistic circuits, and
tensor-train density estimators.</font>

55
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:54,294
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is consistent: at the same
model size,</font>

56
00:03:54,374 --> 00:04:00,021
<font color="#FFFFFF">squared non-monotonic circuits reach higher
log-likelihoods than monotonic circuits across</font>

57
00:04:00,101 --> 00:04:01,867
<font color="#FFFFFF">the density-estimation tasks.</font>

58
00:04:01,947 --> 00:04:06,448
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the two-dimensional ring distributions, plain
squaring already helps,</font>

59
00:04:06,528 --> 00:04:12,621
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it is the negative parameters that let the
model actually carve out the holes in the density.</font>

60
00:04:12,701 --> 00:04:14,784
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on the GPT2 distillation task,</font>

61
00:04:14,864 --> 00:04:19,493
<font color="#FFFFFF">the squared circuits scale better and get closer to
GPT2's own likelihood,</font>

62
00:04:19,573 --> 00:04:24,520
<font color="#FFFFFF">approximating the intractable model more faithfully
than monotonic circuits do.</font>

63
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:29,064
<font color="#FFFFFF">To confirm the gains come from subtraction and not
just squaring,</font>

64
00:04:29,144 --> 00:04:32,655
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors compare against squared monotonic circuits,</font>

65
00:04:32,735 --> 00:04:35,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">which square but keep all parameters non-negative.</font>

66
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,858
<font color="#FFFFFF">The squared non-monotonic circuits still win,</font>

67
00:04:38,938 --> 00:04:42,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the negative parameters are doing the real work.</font>

68
00:04:42,268 --> 00:04:47,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">Two other patterns emerge: binary-tree region
graphs generally beat linear-tree ones,</font>

69
00:04:47,818 --> 00:04:49,566
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the input layer matters,</font>

70
00:04:49,646 --> 00:04:54,594
<font color="#FFFFFF">with splines helping most on continuous data and
embeddings on discrete data.</font>

71
00:04:54,674 --> 00:04:58,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">On some discrete image-mass tasks the advantage narrows.</font>

72
00:04:58,630 --> 00:05:02,621
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers make the point.</font>

73
00:05:02,701 --> 00:05:06,692
<font color="#FFFFFF">Squaring a mixture with K components encodes on the
order of K-squared-over-two pairwise</font>

74
00:05:06,772 --> 00:05:10,763
<font color="#FFFFFF">components while reusing the same K parameters,</font>

75
00:05:10,843 --> 00:05:14,834
<font color="#FFFFFF">which is where the compactness comes from,</font>

76
00:05:14,914 --> 00:05:18,905
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the paper proves this gap over monotonic
circuits is exponential.</font>

77
00:05:18,985 --> 00:05:22,975
<font color="#FFFFFF">Empirically,</font>

78
00:05:23,055 --> 00:05:27,046
<font color="#FFFFFF">the squared non-monotonic circuits post the best
test log-likelihoods among tractable models on several UCI datasets,</font>

79
00:05:27,126 --> 00:05:31,117
<font color="#FFFFFF">for example zero point six two on Power, about
eleven on Gas,</font>

80
00:05:31,197 --> 00:05:35,188
<font color="#FFFFFF">minus twenty point four on Hepmass, and minus
twenty-six point seven on MiniBooNE.</font>

81
00:05:35,268 --> 00:05:39,259
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the language data they climb toward GPT2's own
likelihood of around minus fifty-two,</font>

82
00:05:39,339 --> 00:05:43,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">while monotonic circuits flatten out.</font>

83
00:05:43,710 --> 00:05:45,118
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

84
00:05:45,198 --> 00:05:48,159
<font color="#FFFFFF">Squaring turns subtractive mixtures into valid,</font>

85
00:05:48,239 --> 00:05:53,657
<font color="#FFFFFF">tractable probabilistic models that need
exponentially fewer components than additive</font>

86
00:05:53,737 --> 00:05:56,051
<font color="#FFFFFF">mixtures for the same expressiveness.</font>

87
00:05:56,131 --> 00:06:00,709
<font color="#FFFFFF">Along the way, this one framework unifies
square-root-of-density models,</font>

88
00:06:00,789 --> 00:06:03,103
<font color="#FFFFFF">positive semi-definite kernel models,</font>

89
00:06:03,183 --> 00:06:07,890
<font color="#FFFFFF">and quantum Born machines under a single
property-driven view of circuits,</font>

90
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:09,896
<font color="#FFFFFF">making subtraction a practical,</font>

91
00:06:09,976 --> 00:06:14,230
<font color="#FFFFFF">first-class tool for compact and expressive
probabilistic modeling.</font>
